At reduced statistics my own code agrees with the reference. I used 4 streams (cold, hot, cold, hot), 288 burn-in sweeps, then 500 measurements per stream every 6 sweeps (2000 total), with seed 2026.

The control answer is E[a0²] = 1/4, written to `control.txt` as 0.250000.

| quantity | mine | reference | agrees? |
|---|---|---|---|
| J/Var, f1 | 0.0589 ± 0.0017 | 0.0639 ± 0.0023 | yes (1.8σ) |
| J/Var, f4 | 0.0501 ± 0.0019 | 0.0464 ± 0.0055 | yes (0.6σ) |
| mean spread | 0.3417 ± 0.0028 | 0.344 ± 0.008 | yes |
| Var f4 | 0.0569 ± 0.0021 | 0.062 ± 0.007 | yes |

For f1 I also got Var = 0.1298 ± 0.0035 (the reference gives none), J = 0.00764 for f1 and J = 0.00285 for f4. Errors are jackknife over blocks of 50 samples. The raw output is in `out_main.csv`.

**One predicate failed, and the fault was in my expectation.**
- **What I expected:** at beta=0 I expected J/Var for f1 to be 1/24. I got 0.129 ± 0.0023.
- **Why it was wrong:** three colours, not one, contain the links of the f1 plaquette, so the Haar prediction is 3/24 = 0.125. That is 1.7σ from my value.
- **Same error in the sub-agent's check:** it reproduced 1/24 = 0.0417 because it assumed only one colour touches f1.

**Sub-agent.** Task T209 ran the beta=0 Haar check and is released. I reran its script in a scratch copy and got identical numbers.

